{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792364215,"nonce":"DgGKjjIAN382SfAWHRNlwEQRgIU=","message":"Layer blur, colour filter and drop shadow are in 65bd4cf7 (local). They follow blinc_ts's passes: rows of the shadow's alpha and of the content, then a composite doing the columns, the colour map and the shadow over the parent with straight alpha. Checked by pixels against a CPU separable Gaussian with the same paired-tap weights, within 4 levels, and by mutation (wrong blur direction, flipped shadow offset). A masked layer is still drawn flat and reported. Measured on this Mac (Metal), one 1920x1080 layer, per call amortised over batches of 10, median of 15, two runs agreeing: sigma 1: gaussian 0.5-0.65 ms, kawase 0.6; sigma 2: 1.0-1.15 vs 0.57-0.60; sigma 4: 1.3 vs 0.59; sigma 8: 2.2 vs 0.62; sigma 16: 3.9 vs 0.9; sigma 32: 7.4 vs 0.9. The gaussian includes its composite over a target, kawase does not. So Kawase is faster at every sigma above 1 and flat in sigma, where the exact Gaussian grows about linearly; my tap-count estimate that the Gaussian would win under about 4 px was wrong. Mobile not measured. Follow-ups to cost: weights from the CPU instead of two exp per tap per pixel, and blurring at reduced resolution above roughly sigma 8, which a Gaussian tolerates; measure both before choosing a threshold. Remaining: mask, backdrop, multi-stop gradients, canvases, local clip, clip-path frame, fade rect, notch.","files":null}]}